# THE BUILD CONTEXT. This file is a security control, not a size optimisation.
#
# EVERYTHING EXCLUDED HERE, AND WHY IT MUST NOT REACH A PUBLISHED IMAGE:
#
#   .session-archive/  THE IMPORTANT ONE. It holds this developer's learned
#                      schema memory -- the corrections they made about what
#                      their columns mean, accumulated over months of use. It is
#                      gitignored, so it is not in the repository, which means
#                      `COPY . .` and a naive build context WOULD pick it up
#                      from a working checkout. Baking it into an image
#                      publishes one operator's operational knowledge to
#                      everyone who pulls, and ships it to customers. The
#                      runtime creates its own archive inside the mounted
#                      workspace, which is the correct place for it: per
#                      deployment, per volume, and gone with the volume.
#
#   ui-workspace/      the developer's own data files. Same reasoning: a report
#                      is about somebody's infrastructure and must not ship.
#
#   .git/              full history, which contains every file that was ever
#                      committed including ones later removed, plus remote URLs
#                      and any credential-shaped string ever written and reverted.
#
#   node_modules/ venv/ .venv/  reproducible from the manifests, and in venv's
#                      case enormous and full of host-specific binaries that do
#                      not belong in a portable image at all.
#
#   docs/ tests/ verification/ memory/  not needed to SERVE. The earlier
#                      test-container scaffold copies some of these because its
#                      suite reads them; a serving image has no suite.
#
#   reports/           the gauntlet's own markdown artifacts. The POLICY still
#                      needs a reports/ path to exist -- its rules name one -- so
#                      the Dockerfile creates it empty instead. Path without
#                      contents: the rule describes the tree correctly and none
#                      of the test output ships.
#
# WHAT IS *NOT* EXCLUDED, DELIBERATELY:
#   .pi/       the policy. Excluding it makes the container refuse to start.
#   skills/    the product's skills, including their SKILL.md files. This is why
#              there is no blanket `*.md` rule -- one would strip SKILL.md and
#              leave a skills directory that exists and is empty of meaning.
#   configs/   the shipped schema defaults.

# --- The developer's learned state. Nothing below this line is ever shipped. ---
.session-archive/
**/.session-archive/
ui-workspace/
workspace/

# --- Repository internals ---
.git/
.gitignore
.gitlab-ci.yml
.scratch/
*.log

# --- Reproducible, and full of host binaries ---
node_modules/
venv/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# --- Not needed to serve ---
docs/
tests/
verification/
memory/
reports/

# --- Docker's own files, which would otherwise land in the image ---
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml

# --- Every other dotfile, by default. `.pi` is re-included below because the
#     policy is required to boot, and the failure mode of getting this wrong is
#     silent: a developer's local dot-directory arriving in a published image
#     without anything in the build log mentioning it. ---
.*
!.pi
